# English law of contractual interpretation — *Wood v Capita* [2017] UKSC 24

Source: *Wood v Capita Insurance Services Ltd* [2017] UKSC 24, [2017]
AC 1173 (Supreme Court of the United Kingdom). Pinned URL:
https://www.supremecourt.uk/cases/uksc-2015-0091.html. Doctrine — drift
= Supreme Court overrule. Builds on *Rainy Sky SA v Kookmin Bank*
[2011] UKSC 50 and *Arnold v Britton* [2015] UKSC 36.

ATTRIBUTION (per RULES-ATTRIBUTION.md): Contains public-sector
information licensed under the Open Government Licence v3.0 —
http://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/doc/open-government-licence/version/3/.

## The unitary approach — Lord Hodge

Lord Hodge (with whom Lord Neuberger, Lord Mance, Lord Clarke and Lord
Sumption agreed) reaffirmed the unitary, iterative approach to
contractual interpretation. Quoting paragraphs 10 to 14 verbatim from
the official judgment text:

10. The court's task is to ascertain the objective meaning of the
language which the parties have chosen to express their agreement. It
has long been accepted that this is not a literalist exercise focused
solely on a parsing of the wording of the particular clause but that
the court must consider the contract as a whole and, depending on the
nature, formality and quality of drafting of the contract, give more or
less weight to elements of the wider context in reaching its view as to
that objective meaning.

11. Lord Clarke elegantly summarised the approach to construction in
Rainy Sky at para 21f. In Arnold all of the judgments confirmed the
approach in Rainy Sky. Interpretation is, as Lord Clarke stated in
Rainy Sky (para 21), a unitary exercise; where there are rival meanings,
the court can give weight to the implications of rival constructions by
reaching a view as to which construction is more consistent with
business common sense. But, in striking a balance between the
indications given by the language and the implications of the competing
constructions the court must consider the quality of drafting of the
clause; and it must also be alive to the possibility that one side may
have agreed to something which with hindsight did not serve his
interest. Similarly, the court must not lose sight of the possibility
that a provision may be a negotiated compromise or that the negotiators
were not able to agree more precise terms.

12. This unitary exercise involves an iterative process by which each
suggested interpretation is checked against the provisions of the
contract and its commercial consequences are investigated.

13. Textualism and contextualism are not conflicting paradigms in a
battle for exclusive occupation of the field of contractual
interpretation. Rather, the lawyer and the judge, when interpreting any
contract, can use them as tools to ascertain the objective meaning of
the language which the parties have chosen to express their agreement.
The extent to which each tool will assist the court in its task will
vary according to the circumstances of the particular agreement or
agreements.

14. On the approach to contractual interpretation, Rainy Sky and Arnold
were saying the same thing.

The rule of decision distilled from the unitary approach is structurally:

1. Read the contract as a whole. Where the language used by the
   parties, read in context, is **unambiguous**, the court enforces it
   even where the result appears uncommercial (*Arnold v Britton*).
2. Where rival readings are equally arguable on the language, the
   court adopts the construction more consistent with **business
   common sense**, supported by the **factual matrix** (*Rainy Sky*).
3. Where neither limb resolves the question, the contract is
   **genuinely ambiguous** and the court reaches for further
   interpretive aids (*contra proferentem*, expert evidence on trade
   usage, etc.) outside the scope of this module.

## Encoding

The rule is decision-shaped: the court picks one of {Plain meaning
carries, Business common sense carries, Genuinely ambiguous} and decides
accordingly.

DOCTRINAL NOTE on Stage 2 (post-2026-05 audit): an earlier encoding
required strict conjunction of `common_sense_supports_one_reading AND
factual_matrix_supports_that_reading` for the business-common-sense
limb. Lord Hodge in *Wood v Capita* paras 11–12 frames the unitary
exercise as **iterative balancing**, not strict conjunction —
common-sense and factual-matrix indicators are *both* weighed but
neither is individually necessary. The encoding now uses a balancing
rule: **at least one of** common-sense or factual-matrix must support
a reading, AND the other must not affirmatively contradict it. The
strict-conjunctive view remains expressible by setting both inputs
true; the new rule additionally allows the realistic case where one
indicator is silent or weakly-supporting while the other is
load-bearing.

```catala
declaration enumeration InterpretationLimb:
  -- PlainMeaningCarries
  -- BusinessCommonSenseCarries
  -- GenuinelyAmbiguous

declaration enumeration MatrixSignal:
  -- Supports
  -- Silent
  -- Contradicts

declaration structure ClauseEvidence:
  data clauses_unambiguously_aligned content boolean
  data common_sense_supports_one_reading content boolean
  ## Tri-state: does the factual matrix support the candidate reading,
  ## stay silent on it, or affirmatively cut against it? Lord Hodge's
  ## balancing test does not require positive support from BOTH limbs;
  ## it requires that one limb support the reading and the other not
  ## affirmatively contradict it.
  data factual_matrix_signal content MatrixSignal

declaration structure InterpretationDisposition:
  data limb content InterpretationLimb
  data unambiguous_carries content boolean
  data business_sense_carries content boolean

declaration scope WoodVCapita:
  input evidence content ClauseEvidence
  output disposition content InterpretationDisposition

scope WoodVCapita:
  definition disposition equals
    let plain equals evidence.clauses_unambiguously_aligned in
    let cs equals evidence.common_sense_supports_one_reading in
    let mtx_supports equals
      evidence.factual_matrix_signal = Supports
    in
    let mtx_contradicts equals
      evidence.factual_matrix_signal = Contradicts
    in
    ## Lord Hodge balancing: BCS limb carries when (i) at least one of
    ## common-sense or factual-matrix supports a reading, AND (ii) the
    ## factual matrix does not affirmatively contradict it. A silent
    ## matrix is permissive; a contradicting matrix is a hard cut.
    let bcs equals
      (not plain)
      and (cs or mtx_supports)
      and (not mtx_contradicts)
    in
    let chosen equals
      if plain then PlainMeaningCarries
      else (if bcs then BusinessCommonSenseCarries
            else GenuinelyAmbiguous)
    in
    InterpretationDisposition {
      -- limb: chosen
      -- unambiguous_carries: plain
      -- business_sense_carries: bcs
    }
```

## Application in ADGM and DIFC

ADGM applies this body of law wholesale via the *Application of English
Law Regulations 2015*, so this module is the rule-of-decision for every
ADGM contract dispute. DIFC reaches the same place by analogy through
DIFC Contract Law (DIFC Law No. 6 of 2004), which codifies substantially
the same principles in Articles 49–53 (interpretation of contracts).

Used in:
  trace-05 — ADGMCFI-2024-158 *Xetech v Pulsar* (Heath KC). Clauses
             2(b), 7, 10 unambiguously aligned ⇒ unitary reading carries
             the day; the business-common-sense limb was never reached.

## Tests — three rule-of-decision branches

```catala
#[test] declaration scope TestWoodVCapita_PlainMeaning:
  composite scope WoodVCapita
  output disposition content InterpretationDisposition

scope TestWoodVCapita_PlainMeaning:
  definition disposition equals composite.disposition
  ## trace-05 clause findings: 2(b), 7, 10 all point to
  ## payment-before-transfer. Plain meaning carries (Heath KC, paras 59-60).
  definition composite.evidence equals
    ClauseEvidence {
      -- clauses_unambiguously_aligned: true
      -- common_sense_supports_one_reading: true
      -- factual_matrix_signal: Supports
    }
  assertion disposition.limb = PlainMeaningCarries

#[test] declaration scope TestWoodVCapita_BusinessSenseCarries:
  composite scope WoodVCapita
  output disposition content InterpretationDisposition

scope TestWoodVCapita_BusinessSenseCarries:
  definition disposition equals composite.disposition
  ## Both common-sense and factual-matrix support the same reading.
  definition composite.evidence equals
    ClauseEvidence {
      -- clauses_unambiguously_aligned: false
      -- common_sense_supports_one_reading: true
      -- factual_matrix_signal: Supports
    }
  assertion disposition.limb = BusinessCommonSenseCarries

#[test] declaration scope TestWoodVCapita_BCS_OnCommonSenseAlone:
  composite scope WoodVCapita
  output disposition content InterpretationDisposition

scope TestWoodVCapita_BCS_OnCommonSenseAlone:
  definition disposition equals composite.disposition
  ## Lord Hodge balancing — BCS still carries where common-sense
  ## supports a reading and the factual matrix is silent on it.
  definition composite.evidence equals
    ClauseEvidence {
      -- clauses_unambiguously_aligned: false
      -- common_sense_supports_one_reading: true
      -- factual_matrix_signal: Silent
    }
  assertion disposition.limb = BusinessCommonSenseCarries

#[test] declaration scope TestWoodVCapita_FactualMatrixContradicts:
  composite scope WoodVCapita
  output disposition content InterpretationDisposition

scope TestWoodVCapita_FactualMatrixContradicts:
  definition disposition equals composite.disposition
  ## Common-sense supports a reading but the factual matrix
  ## affirmatively contradicts it: BCS does not carry; ambiguity.
  definition composite.evidence equals
    ClauseEvidence {
      -- clauses_unambiguously_aligned: false
      -- common_sense_supports_one_reading: true
      -- factual_matrix_signal: Contradicts
    }
  assertion disposition.limb = GenuinelyAmbiguous

#[test] declaration scope TestWoodVCapita_Ambiguous:
  composite scope WoodVCapita
  output disposition content InterpretationDisposition

scope TestWoodVCapita_Ambiguous:
  definition disposition equals composite.disposition
  definition composite.evidence equals
    ClauseEvidence {
      -- clauses_unambiguously_aligned: false
      -- common_sense_supports_one_reading: false
      -- factual_matrix_signal: Silent
    }
  assertion disposition.limb = GenuinelyAmbiguous
```
